course: biology
lesson: 310
title: Sharks by kind: great white, tiger, bull and hammerhead
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Sharks are not all the same monster from a film. The great white hunts seals, the tiger shark eats almost anything, the bull shark swims up rivers, and the hammerhead sweeps the sand for hidden stingrays.
explain: Hold a great white tooth in your hand: a triangle with saw edges. Behind each tooth in the jaw wait rows of spares that move forward like a conveyor belt, so a shark can lose and replace thousands of teeth in its life.
explain: Off South Africa a great white rushes up from the dark deep water at a seal on the surface and can launch right out of the water. From below, a seal shows as a dark shape against the bright surface, and so, sadly, can a surfer on a board.
explain: Even so, all sharks together make only about 70 unprovoked bites on people a year worldwide (unprovoked means nobody had poked, caught or fed the shark), and many sharks are now in danger from fishing. Even Peter Benchley, who wrote Jaws, later spent years working to protect them.
fact: A great white has about 300 teeth in rows, triangular with saw edges for slicing seal fat, and new teeth move forward like a conveyor belt; a shark can lose and replace thousands of teeth in its life.
fact: Great whites usually hunt seals, fish and dead whales; most bites on people are thought to be mistakes or test bites, and often the shark swims away. All sharks together make only about 70 unprovoked bites on people a year worldwide, reported.
fact: Off South Africa, great whites rush up from deep water at seals and can launch fully into the air; attacking from below works because the seal cannot see into the dark water.
fact: Peter Benchley, who wrote Jaws, later spent years working to protect sharks.
fact: Great whites keep their muscles, eyes and brain warmer than the sea around them, using special blood vessels that hold the heat in, which helps them swim fast in cold water.
fact: Tiger sharks eat almost anything, from fish, turtles, sea snakes and birds to rubbish like tyres and number plates, reported; their notched teeth cut turtle shells, and young ones have stripes that fade with age.
fact: In Hawaii, tiger sharks gather every summer when young albatrosses learn to fly and drop into the sea.
fact: Bull sharks can live in fresh water as well as the sea, because their kidneys and glands adjust to water without salt; bull sharks lived for years in a lake on a golf course in Australia after a flood.
fact: Bull sharks are short, heavy and strong and live in warm, shallow, murky coastal water near people, so they are among the sharks most often involved in bites; swimmers are told to avoid murky river mouths, especially at dawn and dusk.
fact: A hammerhead's wide head spreads its eyes and electric sensors far apart, sweeping the sand like a metal detector for hidden stingrays and helping it see further around; great hammerheads pin stingrays down with their heads.
fact: Scalloped hammerheads gather in big schools by day near underwater mountains, like off the Galápagos, and split up at night to hunt alone; many hammerheads are endangered by fishing, especially for their fins.
fact: Most sharks have five gill slits on each side, and a few kinds have six or seven; great whites must keep swimming so water flows over their gills, while nurse sharks can rest and pump water over theirs.
exercise: About how many teeth does a great white have in its rows? | About 300 | About 30 | About 3,000 at once | About 32, like an adult person
exercise: How does a shark get new teeth? | Rows of spares move forward like a conveyor belt | It grows one new set of teeth when it is ten years old | It steals teeth from other sharks | It never gets new teeth
exercise: What does a great white usually hunt? | Seals, fish and dead whales | Mostly people swimming at busy beaches | Only seaweed | Only tiny plankton
exercise: About how many unprovoked shark bites on people are reported each year worldwide? | About 70 | About 7,000 | About 70,000 | None at all
exercise: Why does a great white attack seals from below? | The seal cannot see into the dark water beneath it | Sharks cannot swim anywhere near the surface of the sea | Seals only look downwards | The shark wants to see the sky
exercise: What did Peter Benchley, who wrote Jaws, do later in life? | He worked to protect sharks | He became a shark hunter | He wrote that sharks eat boats | He kept a great white in his pool
exercise: How does a great white keep its muscles warm in cold water? | Special blood vessels hold the heat in | It grows a thick layer of fur in winter | It swims near volcanoes | It shivers its muscles like a bumblebee
exercise: What is a tiger shark famous for eating? | Almost anything, even tyres and number plates | Only one kind of tiny silver fish | Only ripe fruit that falls from trees into the sea | Nothing, it lives on sunlight
exercise: Why is it called a tiger shark? | Young ones have stripes, which fade with age | It lives in the jungle, along the rivers | It roars like a tiger when it hunts its prey | It has orange fur
exercise: What do tiger sharks wait for in Hawaii each summer? | Young albatrosses dropping into the sea while learning to fly | Ships full of food | Tourists who throw them fish from the boats | Snow falling on the sea
exercise: What is special about bull sharks? | They can live in fresh water as well as the sea | They live only in deep, cold oceans far from any coast | They are the smallest sharks | They breathe air like whales
exercise: Where did bull sharks live for years in Australia after a flood? | In a lake on a golf course | In a big hotel swimming pool | In a desert | In a fish tank at the local school
exercise: Why are swimmers told to avoid murky river mouths at dawn and dusk? | Bull sharks hunt in that warm, shallow, murky water | The water is far too cold for swimming at those times | Sharks only sleep at noon | Fish bite at dawn
exercise: How does a hammerhead use its wide head? | Its spread-out eyes and sensors find stingrays hidden in sand | To hammer open oyster shells on the rocks | To fight boats | To float on the top of the sea like a raft
exercise: What do scalloped hammerheads do by day near the Galápagos? | Gather in big schools | Hunt alone in the dark | Sleep on the sea bed | Swim up rivers
exercise: Why are many hammerheads endangered? | Fishing, especially for their fins | Too many stingrays | Warm water makes them shrink until they starve | They forget how to swim
exercise: How many gill slits do most sharks have on each side? | Five | Two | Ten | One
exercise: Why must a great white keep swimming? | So water keeps flowing over its gills | So it does not sink into the hot lava under the sea | So it never gets bored | So its teeth do not fall out
test: Count them: a great white has 5 gill slits on each side. How many on both sides together? | 10 | 5 | 7 | 14
test: Count them: 3 hammerheads swim past. How many eyes, and how many tails? | 6 eyes and 3 tails | 3 eyes and 3 tails | 6 eyes and 6 tails | 12 eyes and 3 tails
test: Count them: a shark loses 2 teeth a week. About how many in a year of 52 weeks? | 104 | 54 | 26 | 520
test: In Finding Nemo, Bruce the great white says «fish are friends, not food». What do real great whites usually eat? | Seals, fish and dead whales | Only seaweed salad | Nothing but people who swim | Only other sharks, even their own young
test: «sharks hunt humans, saw Jaws, never going in the sea» What is the best reply? | Bites are rare, about 70 a year worldwide, many of them mistakes; follow beach safety | Sharks hunt people every day | Every beach has a great white waiting | Sharks only bite people in films, never in real life, so swim anywhere you like at any time
test: Why might a surfer lying on a board look like a seal to a shark below? | From below both make a dark shape against the bright surface | Surfers smell exactly like seals in the water | Sharks think boards are fish | Surfers bark like seals
test: «a shark in a lake? nah, sharks need the sea innit» What is right? | Bull sharks can live in fresh water and have lived in a lake in Australia | No shark can survive one minute in fresh water, it would die straight away | Only goldfish sharks live in lakes | Sharks in lakes are always toys
test: What would happen if a great white stopped swimming for a long time? | Too little water would flow over its gills | It would grow legs | It would breathe much better and feel rested | It would warm up and float
test: A tiger shark is cut open and a number plate is inside. Why is this not so surprising? | Tiger sharks eat almost anything, rubbish included | Number plates are its favourite food | Tiger sharks collect cars | Somebody hid it there as a joke before it was caught
test: A scientist sees scalloped hammerheads together at noon but alone at midnight. What explains it? | They school by day and split up at night to hunt alone | They are lost at night | They sleep together in big groups at midnight | They are two different species
test: Why might young tiger sharks need stripes more than big old ones? | Small sharks have more enemies, and stripes may help them hide | Stripes make them swim slower | Old sharks hate stripes and bite any young shark that has them | Stripes keep the water warm
test: «why would a shark have its eyes on the ends of a hammer, looks dumb» What is the best answer? | Wide-set eyes and sensors help it look around and find buried stingrays | It is a deformity that makes life harder | It uses the hammer to build reefs | Its eyes are only for show
exam: Identify the principal prey of the great white shark. | Seals, fish and the carcasses of whales | Human beings frequenting beaches | Seaweed and other marine vegetation | Exclusively other species of shark
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the bull shark? | It is capable of inhabiting fresh water as well as the sea | It is incapable of surviving outside the deep ocean | It is the largest predatory fish in existence | It breathes atmospheric air by means of lungs
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the head of the hammerhead shark. | Its width separates the eyes and electrical sensors, assisting the detection of concealed prey | Its width serves solely to break the shells of molluscs | Its width is a defect that impedes hunting | Its width enables the shark to remain afloat without swimming, in the manner of a raft or a plank
exam: Ascertain the approximate number of unprovoked bites on human beings attributed to all sharks annually throughout the world. | Approximately seventy | Approximately seven thousand | Approximately seventy thousand | Approximately seven hundred thousand
